386→                        <div id="content">Let $S$ be the set of all $m\geq 1$ such that there exists a prime $p\not\equiv 1\pmod{m}$ such that $m!+1\equiv 0\pmod{p}$. Does\[\lim \frac{\lvert S\cap [1,x]\rvert}{x}\]exist? What is it?<br><br>Similarly, if $P$ is the set of all primes $p$ such that there exists an $m$ with $p\not\equiv 1\pmod{m}$ such that $m!+1\equiv 0\pmod{p}$, then does\[\lim \frac{\lvert P\cap [1,x]\rvert}{\pi(x)}\]exist? What is it?</div>
387→                        <div id="problem_id"><a href="/1074">#1074</a>: <a href="#cite-HaSu02-1074" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('HaSu02','1074'); return false;">[HaSu02]</a></div>
